From 05ef279a97ae635f76b44c82569458ae466b2928 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maciej Kowalik <konnak@o2.pl>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:30:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: a prompt chunk runs a slice of layers at a time
 between decode rounds

TF_GLM_FILL_SLICE_MS (milliseconds, 0 or unset: off, today whole chunks) cuts a prompt chunk by layers
as well as chunks: a lane fill step runs one slice of about that GPU cost, settles the stream and returns to
the host loop, whose decode rounds pay the fill share debt at the slice grain (longStep times the call, so
the smaller grain needs no lane_host change). runRange is run over layers [j0, j1): the prolog and the finish
belong to the whole range alone, the kernels, their order and the commit staying where they were, so kept
states keep their 4,096-row grid and the answers their bits. Rank 0 picks each slice end (sliceHi: the first
chunk with slicing calibrates whole, a CUDA event a layer -- no server-startup probe as the Python family
needed -- then LayerCosts, that family class ported: row bands, an EWMA scale, greedy cuts within the budget,
a slower layer alone, the fewest slices with each cut near the mean), and the new chunkpart step carries it to
the other ranks, which hold their own duped chunk and run the same range. A cancelled fill drops its suspended
chunk without the commit (dropPartialOf and snapshots.interrupted, as a gate cancel keeps the states marked so
far); another stream step advances a foreign chunk by one slice, so a decode round never waits on more.
